--TEST--
A throw no `catch` handles ends the program at status 1 with standard output intact, and the floor writes one record carrying the class, the message and a node per frame, innermost first — `rule:errors/propagation`, `rule:errors/engine-floor`, `rule:errors/record-producers`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:errors/an-uncaught-throw
// The two halves of this feature that a program can observe from outside it:
// what standard output keeps, and what the floor writes when the throw
// reaches the root. `rule:errors/propagation` is the path between them.

class Ledger {
    public static function post(int $entry): int {
        throw new RuntimeError("entry " . $entry . " does not balance");
    }
}

class Batch {
    public static function run(): int {
        return Ledger::post(4);
    }
}

// Written before the throw, so it survives the ending: the floor writes to
// its own sink and takes nothing back.
echo "posting 4\n";

// Two frames deep, and no arm anywhere catches it. `rule:errors/engine-floor`
// is the tier that reports it, because no `[log] handler` is configured here.
echo Batch::run(), "\n";

// Nothing after the throw runs, which is what the absent third line asserts.
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--
posting 4
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
{"level":"error","msg":"entry 4 does not balance","fields":{"class":"RuntimeError"},"nodes":[{"function":"Ledger::post","file":"%s","line":%d},{"function":"Batch::run","file":"%s","line":%d},{"function":"<script>","file":"%s","line":%d}]}
